<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/10th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Brunanburh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/937">937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Brunanburh was fought in 937 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a>, king of the English, and an alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-guthfrithson">Olaf Guthfrithson</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-ii-of-scotland">Constantine II of Scotland</a> and Owain of Strathclyde. Æthelstan's victory confirmed his rule over a united English kingdom and was celebrated in an Old English poem preserved in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a>. The site of the battle is unknown, though the Wirral is a leading candidate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-brunanburh.jpg" alt="Battle of Brunanburh" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See description · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athelstan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> at York in 927, Constantine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hywel-dda">Hywel Dda</a> of Deheubarth, Ealdred I of Bamburgh and Owain of Strathclyde accepted his overlordship at Eamont, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penrith">Penrith</a>. He invaded Scotland in 934 with a large military and naval force; John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a> gave as the cause Constantine's violation of the treaty of 927. The army harassed the Scots as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kincardineshire">Kincardineshire</a> and the navy as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caithness">Caithness</a>, but was never engaged. It then became clear that Æthelstan could be beaten only by an alliance of his enemies, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-guthfrithson">Olaf Guthfrithson</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, led one with Constantine II and Owain; according to John of Worcester, Constantine was Olaf's father-in-law.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In August 937 Olaf sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> to join Constantine and Owain. Æthelstan's army went north through Mercia and met them at Brunanburh. According to the Old English poem in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a>, the English fought all day and finally forced the invaders to flee, pursuing them until nightfall. Olaf escaped by ship to Dublin and Constantine to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>; Owain's fate is not mentioned. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a> call the battle great, lamentable and horrible, and record that several thousands of Norsemen fell, among them five kings and seven earls of Olaf's army. Constantine's son died, and so did two of Æthelstan's cousins, Ælfwine and Æthelwine.</p><p>More than forty medieval texts mention the battle. Egil's Saga, written in Old Norse in the early 13th century, gives detail found nowhere else, but historians such as Sarah Foot doubt it is a reliable narrative. Later chroniclers differ on matters such as the size of Olaf's fleet, given as 615 ships by Symeon of Durham, and on whether Constantine was killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The victory preserved the unity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and Michael Livingston calls it the moment when Englishness came of age. Alfred Smyth ranked it above every other single battle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> history before Hastings, though he thinks its effects beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a>'s reign have been overstated. Alex Woolf calls it a pyrrhic victory: the campaign ended in stalemate, and after Æthelstan died in 939 Olaf became king of Northumbria without resistance. The Northumbrians submitted to southern kingship only in 954.</p><p>The site is unknown. More than forty places have been proposed, though most historians think northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> most plausible. Bromborough on the Wirral is the only surviving place name derived from Old English Brunanburh, and a local volunteer group found a field there with many artefacts, but others dispute the evidence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Brunanburh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He styled himself king of the English, and in 937 he defeated a coalition of Scots, Strathclyde Britons and Dublin Vikings at the <b>Battle of Brunanburh</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
